Attribution
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The MIT License already requires you to keep the copyright notice and
the LICENSE file when you redistribute this code (with or without
modifications). In addition, when you deploy a fork of Crucible
publicly or under a different name, we kindly request that you keep a
visible attribution link to the original project in the deployed UI.

The default footer already includes such a link:

    "Powered by Crucible -- github.com/Zigm4/crucible"

If you keep that line (or move it elsewhere in your UI but keep it
visible), you're already done.

If you remove or hide it: nothing in the MIT License legally forbids
that, but it goes against the spirit of why this code exists. Crucible
was built specifically so that smart-contract communities are NEVER
locked to a single client again. Stripping attribution off a fork
breaks that chain in a small but real way.

